    "tags": ["HCI", "Music", "Input", "Touch", "Capacitive"],
    "overview": "The Omnichord is an electronic musical instrument introduced in 1981 by the Suzuki Musical Instrument Corporation. Conceived as an electronic autoharp, it allows users to play harp-like arpeggios through an electronic strum plate — a capacitive touch strip branded 'Sonic Strings' — while selecting chords from a grid of buttons. The original OM-27 featured 27 chord buttons, preset drum machine rhythms, and a single 'harp' sound. Successive models added more chords (up to 84), MIDI connectivity, a chord sequencer, and multiple instrument voices. Production continued through the OM-300 in 1996, with a revival model (OM-108) released in 2024.",

    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Origins",
        "text": "Suzuki, known primarily for educational musical instruments like the Melodion, created the Omnichord to democratize music-making. It was aimed at people without musical experience who might be intimidated by traditional keyboard instruments. The first model, OM-27, launched in 1981 alongside the Tronichord (also known as the Portachord). The OM-27 featured only 27 chord shapes and a single harp timbre. By 1984, the OM-36 and OM-84 expanded the chord palette, and the OM-100 repositioned the strum plate for comfort while adding guitar, piano, banjo, organ, flute, brass, and synth voices."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Interaction Model",
        "text": "The Omnichord requires a distinctive bimanual coordination unlike any traditional instrument. The left hand presses chord buttons (major, minor, 7th) arranged in a grid. The right hand strums the capacitive touch plate — the 'Sonic Strings.' The touch strip maps chord-degree arpeggios across a 4-octave span, and the notes are always in key with the selected chord button. You cannot play a wrong note; the instrument constrains output to the chosen harmonic context. A built-in rhythm machine and auto-bass line provide accompaniment. The result is an instrument where the physical gesture (strumming) is preserved from acoustic autoharps, but the interaction surface is entirely electronic — a touch-sensitive ribbon replacing strings."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Cultural Impact",
        "text": "The Omnichord was used by David Bowie (2001 Concert for New York City), Brian Eno and Daniel Lanois (slowed-down Omnichord on 'Deep Blue Day' from Apollo: Atmospheres and Soundtracks), Eurythmics ('Love Is a Stranger'), Talking Heads, Devo, and The Human League. Gorillaz used the OM-300's 'Rock 1' preset for their 2001 hit 'Clint Eastwood.' Tiny Tim claimed his 1985 single was 'the first recording ever played entirely on the Suzuki Omnichord.' In 2024, Meshell Ndegeocello's album The Omnichord Real Book won the inaugural Grammy Award for Best Alternative Jazz Album."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Legacy",
        "text": "The Omnichord was one of the earliest mass-market consumer devices where a capacitive touch strip served as the primary expressive surface — years before touchscreens became ubiquitous. Its design philosophy — constraining the musical output to guarantee pleasant results regardless of skill — anticipated everything from Guitar Hero to GarageBand's Smart Instruments. The 2024 OM-108 revival, with its analog circuitry and model emulations, confirms the instrument's enduring appeal across generations of musicians and non-musicians alike."
      }
    ],

